WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.133 --> 00:00:17.299
My name is Erich Jarvis. I'm an associate professor at Duke University Medical Center.

00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:21.066
I'm also a Howard Hughes Medical Institute Investigator.

00:00:21.067 --> 00:00:27.432
I'm going to be talking to you today about why I became a scientist

00:00:27.433 --> 00:00:29.332
and how I became a scientist.

00:00:29.333 --> 00:00:32.499
Just to let you know, right now I run a laboratory

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:38.666
that roughly has anywhere from 15 to 20 persons,

00:00:38.667 --> 00:00:42.466
depending on the semester, and we study brain pathways for vocal learning.

00:00:42.467 --> 00:00:45.232
But that's not how I began.

00:00:45.233 --> 00:00:50.132
I grew up in New York City, mostly in the Bronx and Queens,

00:00:50.133 --> 00:00:55.632
and I grew up in a family that came from diverse economic backgrounds,

00:00:55.633 --> 00:01:03.966
from being relatively poor, middle class, and we always were told to have a dream in life,

00:01:03.967 --> 00:01:08.266
to make your life better, to do something important.

00:01:08.267 --> 00:01:12.099
But, most of my family was into the performing arts, particularly on my mother's side.

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:20.866
And so, we were all dreaming to be famous artists, singers, dancers, and so forth.

00:01:20.867 --> 00:01:30.899
And, to do this with a passion. And so, when I was a youngster in elementary school

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:32.399
and eventually going to high school,

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:37.832
I auditioned for the high school of performing arts and got to be a dance major there.

00:01:37.833 --> 00:01:43.699
And then also was at the Joffrey Ballet School in New York City

00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:48.299
and the Alvin Ailey Dance Company, and I was pushing along to be a serious dancer.

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:51.166
And I would dance 6 hours a day --

00:01:51.167 --> 00:01:56.366
three hours at school, three hours at once of these dance schools --

00:01:56.367 --> 00:01:58.266
and, in between, would have academic classes

00:01:58.267 --> 00:02:04.199
like math, biology, reading, writing, and so on.

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:07.099
At some point, towards the end of my high school years,

00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:12.332
I started really thinking about, "Is this really the career I want to pursue?"

00:02:12.333 --> 00:02:18.999
And I was doing ok. For instance, my senior year, towards the end of that year,

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:22.732
I was asked to audition for Alvin Ailey Dance School.

00:02:22.733 --> 00:02:25.199
I mean, the Company.

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:28.466
I was asking myself, "What do I really want to do in life?"

00:02:28.467 --> 00:02:32.099
I started asking that question because my mother always said to me,

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:33.899
"It doesn't really matter what you do."

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:37.132
"What's most important that what you do, you do it well."

00:02:37.133 --> 00:02:39.599
And, she would then add that,

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:42.499
"I'd like you to do something that has a positive impact on society."

00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:47.466
And I thought, "What can I do that has a positive impact on society?"

00:02:47.467 --> 00:02:52.632
I felt I could do that better as a scientist than I could do that as a dancer.

00:02:52.633 --> 00:02:57.866
So, at my senior dance concert, right at the end of the dance concert,

00:02:57.867 --> 00:03:00.032
I'm making this decision here, and I said,

00:03:00.033 --> 00:03:02.732
"You know what, I'd like to go ahead and be a scientist."

00:03:02.733 --> 00:03:05.399
And I didn't have any scientific background or training at all.

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:07.799
I had biology classes, which I really liked.

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:12.266
So, I applied to different colleges, got into Hunter College,

00:03:12.267 --> 00:03:15.299
and actually had to take remedial courses

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:20.699
because they said I wasn't prepared enough to take the advanced academic classes.

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:26.166
So, I took a summer of remedial classes, and then really started

00:03:26.167 --> 00:03:31.032
intensively studying biology and mathematics, like I was studying dance.

00:03:31.033 --> 00:03:33.466
And the reason why I was studying biology and math

00:03:33.467 --> 00:03:38.099
is because I felt I wasn't sure if I really liked biology better

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:44.332
(or, let's say, that is the understanding of how biological organisms work)

00:03:44.333 --> 00:03:45.899
or the origins of the universe.

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:48.032
Those are my two major interests.

00:03:48.033 --> 00:03:49.399
So, I decided to do both.

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:52.466
And I thought math would actually help me think better.

00:03:52.467 --> 00:04:01.566
So, then, after my first year, I got into a program that helps under-represented minorities

00:04:01.567 --> 00:04:04.366
get into scientific research.

00:04:04.367 --> 00:04:08.599
And this was a great advantage that I discovered later offset

00:04:08.600 --> 00:04:11.899
a disadvantage that I had that I didn't realize many other people had.

00:04:11.900 --> 00:04:15.499
And then, I really worked with a passion in the lab.

00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:17.366
I would sometimes spend the night in the lab,

00:04:17.367 --> 00:04:20.499
do experiments on the weekends, on the holidays.

00:04:20.500 --> 00:04:24.599
And, people would call up, "Where is Erich? Where is Erich? Where can we find him?"

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:26.999
In the lab. That's where he was.

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:34.132
And I fell in love with doing scientific research in the lab, like I did as a dancer.

00:04:34.133 --> 00:04:38.466
And people often ask me, "Well how did you make that switch?"

00:04:38.467 --> 00:04:40.432
"Aren't those two different fields very different?"

00:04:40.433 --> 00:04:43.332
And I discovered at the time that no, they're not.

00:04:43.333 --> 00:04:46.766
Both of them require a lot of discipline.

00:04:46.767 --> 00:04:49.732
Both of them require a lot of hard work.

00:04:49.733 --> 00:04:53.299
Both of them require acceptance of failure.

00:04:53.300 --> 00:04:58.832
That is, you have do a choreographed dance over and over and over again

00:04:58.833 --> 00:05:01.832
until you get it right, just like an experiment.

00:05:01.833 --> 00:05:05.099
Sometimes, if you submit a paper for a publication, it gets rejected.

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:08.466
Sometimes, you go for an audition, you get rejected.

00:05:08.467 --> 00:05:12.199
So, you have to learn how to accept rejection and try over again.

00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:18.099
And, importantly, both of them require a good level of creativity.

00:05:18.100 --> 00:05:26.566
As an artist, you need to be creative to have your art be appreciated by others,

00:05:26.567 --> 00:05:28.399
you need to come up with something new.

00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:34.232
Same thing for science. You need to be creative to understand how nature works.

00:05:34.233 --> 00:05:41.266
And so, to me, dance and science was a natural merge of two different fields.

00:05:41.267 --> 00:05:48.899
Then, when I was at Hunter College, where I did my undergraduate

00:05:48.900 --> 00:05:52.866
scientific research and also academic training,

00:05:52.867 --> 00:05:58.466
I decided, "What do I want to do particularly within science for my future career?"

00:05:58.467 --> 00:06:03.399
And I decided, "You know what? I really am interested in two questions."

00:06:03.400 --> 00:06:08.932
One is the origins of the universe, and the other is how the brain works.

00:06:08.933 --> 00:06:15.499
And, in trying to choose between those two, I chose to study how the brain works.

00:06:15.500 --> 00:06:19.699
Why? Because I felt it was more rooted in my dance background.

00:06:19.700 --> 00:06:22.032
That is, more rooted in something here in the Earth.

00:06:22.033 --> 00:06:26.932
And, as a result of that, I applied to different graduate schools,

00:06:26.933 --> 00:06:29.266
and I got into a number of them because,

00:06:29.267 --> 00:06:32.099
from all the hard work I did as an undergraduate student,

00:06:32.100 --> 00:06:35.532
I was able to publish 6 papers in scientific journals,

00:06:35.533 --> 00:06:40.166
either as first author papers or collaborative papers with others in the lab.

00:06:40.167 --> 00:06:42.266
And that helped me become competitive

00:06:42.267 --> 00:06:44.166
to get into some good graduate school programs.

00:06:44.167 --> 00:06:48.332
And I was trying to decide, do I want to do an MD-PhD or a PhD

00:06:48.333 --> 00:06:50.899
to answer this question about brain function?

00:06:50.900 --> 00:06:57.032
And I decided on the PhD because usually the PhDs are the folks

00:06:57.033 --> 00:06:59.966
who actually make the discoveries to understand how things work

00:06:59.967 --> 00:07:01.699
or the cures for diseases.

00:07:01.700 --> 00:07:04.466
So I went to Rockefeller University,

00:07:04.467 --> 00:07:09.932
as a graduate student, and I began to study the songbird brain,

00:07:09.933 --> 00:07:14.366
and discovered genes that are regulated in those areas

00:07:14.367 --> 00:07:21.199
that either control the neural circuits for forming learned vocalizations,

00:07:21.200 --> 00:07:23.999
or at least producing them.

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:28.966
And, to make a long story short, to skip a number of years later

00:07:28.967 --> 00:07:34.799
to now being an associate professor at Duke University Medical Center,

00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:37.932
teaching medical students on how the brain works,

00:07:37.933 --> 00:07:42.432
I recently discovered with my collaborators

00:07:42.433 --> 00:07:45.966
that the brain pathways that control vocal learning abilities

00:07:45.967 --> 00:07:52.432
are embedded within brain areas that control motor and sequenced behavior,

00:07:52.433 --> 00:07:54.799
including potentially learning how to dance.

00:07:54.800 --> 00:07:59.632
And so now, I'm actually coming full circle to bringing my scientific career

00:07:59.633 --> 00:08:04.666
and my former dance training together because those two brain circuits

00:08:04.667 --> 00:08:06.432
seem to be related to each other.

00:08:06.433 --> 00:08:10.466
And I hope in the future to discover what that relationship really is.

00:08:10.467 --> 00:08:14.666
And I think the answer is going to tell us how brain pathways

00:08:14.667 --> 00:08:17.399
for complex circuits evolved in general.

00:08:17.400 --> 01:00:00.000
And this would be a natural, nice merging of art and science.

